          "text": "     1→# CLAUDE.md\n     2→\n     3→This file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.\n     4→\n     5→## Project Overview\n     6→\n     7→A TypeScript monorepo for building AI-powered React applications with WebSocket-based AI agent integration. The library enables React components to expose tools (functions) to Claude AI, which can then call them via natural language prompts.\n     8→\n     9→## Development Commands\n    10→\n    11→### Setup\n    12→```bash\n    13→bun install                    # Install dependencies\n    14→```\n    15→\n    16→### Development\n    17→```bash\n    18→export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=...  # Required for server\n    19→bun run start:server           # Start AI server (port 8080)\n    20→bun run dev                    # Start example app with hot reload\n    21→```\n    22→\n    23→The server must be running before starting the example app. The example app runs at http://localhost:3000.\n    24→\n    25→### Building\n    26→```bash\n    27→bun run build                  # Build all packages (client, server, example)\n    28→bun run build:client           # Build @use-ai/client only\n    29→bun run build:server           # Build @use-ai/server only\n    30→```\n    31→\n    32→Build process for packages: Bun bundles code → TypeScript generates type declarations.\n    33→\n    34→### Testing\n    35→```bash\n    36→bun test                       # Run all tests\n    37→```\n    38→\n    39→## Architecture\n    40→\n    41→### Monorepo Structure\n    42→- `packages/client/` - `@use-ai/client`: React hooks library for AI integrations\n    43→- `packages/server/` - `@use-ai/server`: WebSocket server for AI agent hosting\n    44→- `apps/example/` - Example AI-powered todo list demonstrating the library\n    45→\n    46→### Core Data Flow\n    47→\n    48→1. **Tool Registration**: Client registers tools (defined with Zod schemas) via WebSocket to server\n    49→2. **User Prompt**: User sends natural language prompt through UI\n    50→3. **AI Processing**: Server forwards prompt + tool definitions to Claude API\n    51→4. **Tool Execution**: Claude returns tool_use blocks → Server sends `tool_call` to client → Client executes tool locally → Client sends result back\n    52→5. **Response**: Server sends tool results to Claude → Claude generates final response → Server streams to client\n    53→\n    54→### Key Architectural Patterns\n    55→\n    56→#### Client-Side Tool Execution\n    57→- Tools are **defined and executed on the client** (where application state lives)\n    58→- Server acts as **coordinator** between Claude API and client tools\n    59→- This enables tools to access React state, browser APIs, and local data directly\n    60→\n    61→#### WebSocket Protocol\n    62→All messages follow `{ type: string, data: object }` format:\n    63→\n    64→**Client → Server:**\n    65→- `register_tools`: Send tool definitions (JSON Schema format)\n    66→- `prompt`: Send user prompt with optional conversation ID\n    67→- `tool_response`: Return tool execution result for a specific tool call\n    68→\n    69→**Server → Client:**\n    70→- `tool_call`: Request tool execution with `{ toolCallId, name, input }`\n    71→- `response`: Final AI response with conversation ID\n    72→- `error`: Error messages\n    73→\n    74→#### Promise-Based Tool Coordination\n    75→The server uses promises to synchronously wait for async tool execution across the WebSocket boundary:\n    76→```typescript\n    77→// Server creates promise and stores resolver\n    78→const promise = new Promise(resolve => {\n    79→  session.pendingToolCalls.set(toolCallId, resolve);\n    80→});\n    81→// Send tool_call to client\n    82→this.sendMessage(ws, 'tool_call', { toolCallId, name, input });\n    83→// Wait for client to execute and send tool_response\n    84→const result = await promise;\n    85→```\n    86→\n    87→#### Conversation History Management\n    88→- Server maintains **full conversation history per WebSocket session**\n    89→- History includes user messages, assistant messages (with tool_use blocks), and tool results\n    90→- Enables multi-turn conversations with context preservation\n    91→- History is lost on disconnect (session-based, not persisted to database)\n    92→\n    93→#### Tool Aggregation Pattern\n    94→- `UseAIProvider` maintains a registry of tools from all mounted `useAI` hooks\n    95→- Tools from multiple components are merged into a single set\n    96→- Each hook gets a unique ID for registration/cleanup\n    97→- Enables compositional tool registration across component tree\n    98→\n    99→### Tool Definition System\n   100→\n   101→Tools are defined using `defineTool()` with Zod schemas:\n   102→\n   103→```typescript\n   104→import { defineTool } from '@use-ai/client';\n   105→import { z } from 'zod';\n   106→\n   107→const addTodo = defineTool(\n   108→  'Add a new todo item to the list',\n   109→  z.object({ text: z.string() }),\n   110→  (input) => {\n   111→    // Implementation with typed input\n   112→    return { success: true, message: 'Todo added' };\n   113→  }\n   114→);\n   115→```\n   116→\n   117→**Under the hood:**\n   118→- Zod schema is converted to JSON Schema (via `zod-to-json-schema`) for Claude API\n   119→- Original Zod schema is retained for runtime validation\n   120→- `_execute()` method validates input with Zod before calling function\n   121→- TypeScript types are inferred from Zod schema\n   122→\n   123→### State Synchronization Pattern\n   124→\n   125→The example app demonstrates a pub/sub pattern for keeping UI in sync when tools mutate state:\n   126→\n   127→```typescript\n   128→const listeners = new Set<() => void>();\n   129→\n   130→export function subscribeTodos(listener: () => void) {\n   131→  listeners.add(listener);\n   132→  return () => listeners.delete(listener);\n   133→}\n   134→\n   135→function notifyListeners() {\n   136→  listeners.forEach(fn => fn());\n   137→}\n   138→\n   139→// Tools call notifyListeners() after state mutations\n   140→```\n   141→\n   142→This enables both AI operations and manual UI operations to update the same state.\n   143→\n   144→## TypeScript Configuration\n   145→\n   146→- **Strict mode enabled**: All type checks enforced\n   147→- **Module system**: ESM throughout (`\"type\": \"module\"` in all package.json files)\n   148→- **Module resolution**: `\"bundler\"` (Bun-specific)\n   149→- **Target**: ES2022\n   150→- Never use `any` type unless unavoidable\n   151→\n   152→## Dependencies\n   153→\n   154→### Client Package\n   155→- `zod` (v4.x): Schema validation and JSON Schema generation\n   156→- `zod-to-json-schema`: Converts Zod schemas to JSON Schema for Claude API\n   157→- `react` (peer dependency): ^18.0.0\n   158→\n   159→### Server Package\n   160→- `@anthropic-ai/sdk`: Claude API integration\n   161→- `ws`: WebSocket server implementation\n   162→\n   163→## Important Implementation Details\n   164→\n   165→### Connection Management\n   166→- Client auto-reconnects on disconnect (5 attempts with exponential backoff)\n   167→- Each `useAI` hook creates a dedicated WebSocket client instance\n   168→- Provider monitors connection status for UI feedback\n   169→\n   170→### Error Handling\n   171→- Tool execution errors are caught and sent back to Claude as `tool_result` content\n   172→- Zod validation errors propagate as tool execution errors\n   173→- Server errors sent as `error` messages to client\n   174→- Client handles errors via optional `onError` callback in `useAI` hook\n   175→\n   176→### Build System\n   177→- Bun is used for both runtime and bundling\n   178→- Package builds: `bun build` creates bundle → `tsc --emitDeclarationOnly` generates types\n   179→- Example app build: `bun build --target browser --minify`\n   180→- All packages output ESM modules\n   181→\n   182→### Environment Variables\n   183→- `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`: Required for server to communicate with Claude API\n   184→- Server runs on port 8080 (hardcoded in `packages/server/src/start.ts`)\n   185→\n   186→### File Naming Conventions\n   187→- React components: PascalCase (e.g., `UseAIFloatingButton.tsx`)\n   188→- Utilities and hooks: camelCase (e.g., `defineTool.ts`, `useAI.ts`)\n   189→- Main entry points: `index.ts` or `index.tsx`\n   190→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n",
