            "content": "# use-ai\n\n> Frontend AI toolkit for React applications\n\nA TypeScript library that enables React applications to integrate AI chat functionality similar to Notion AI. Users can control UI components through natural language, with AI executing actions via tools defined directly in React components.\n\n## 🎯 Overview\n\n`use-ai` allows you to add an AI chat widget to any page where users can perform actions using natural language. The AI can access and manipulate component state through tools you define, enabling powerful interactions like:\n\n- \"Add a shopping list for making tonkotsu ramen\" → Creates multiple todo items\n- \"Delete all completed todos\" → Removes completed items in bulk\n- \"Change the color of row 3 to blue\" → Updates specific component state\n\nThe library is production-ready with built-in rate limiting, prompt caching, and security considerations.\n\n## 📦 Structure\n\n```\n.\n├── packages/\n│   ├── client/          # @use-ai/client - React hooks library for AI integrations\n│   └── server/          # @use-ai/server - WebSocket server for AI agent hosting\n└── apps/\n    └── example/         # Example AI-powered todo list app\n```\n\n## 🚀 Quick Start\n\n### Installation\n\n```bash\nbun install\n```\n\n### Development Setup\n\n1. Start the AI server (requires `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`):\n```bash\nexport ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=your_api_key_here\nbun run start:server\n```\n\nThe server starts on port 8080.\n\n2. In another terminal, run the example app:\n```bash\nbun run dev\n```\n\nVisit http://localhost:3000 to see the AI-powered todo list in action.\n\n## 💡 Core Concepts\n\n### The `useAI` Hook\n\nThe fundamental building block for adding AI capabilities to any React component:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useAI, defineTool } from '@use-ai/client';\nimport { z } from 'zod';\n\nfunction TodoList() {\n  const [todos, setTodos] = useState<Todo[]>([]);\n\n  // Define a tool the AI can call\n  const addTodo = defineTool(\n    'Add a new todo item to the list',\n    z.object({\n      text: z.string().describe('The text content of the todo item'),\n    }),\n    (input) => {\n      const newTodo = {\n        id: Date.now(),\n        text: input.text.trim(),\n        completed: false,\n      };\n      setTodos(prev => [...prev, newTodo]);\n      return { success: true, message: `Added todo: \"${input.text}\"` };\n    }\n  );\n\n  // Register tools and provide current state to AI\n  useAI({\n    tools: { addTodo },\n    prompt: `Current todos: ${JSON.stringify(todos)}`\n  });\n\n  return (/* your UI */);\n}\n```\n\n### Component State via `prompt`\n\nThe `prompt` argument is how you provide the AI with current component state:\n\n```tsx\nuseAI({\n  tools: { updateLabel, incrementCounter, changeColor },\n  prompt: `Current state - Label: \"${label}\", Counter: ${counter}, Color: \"${color}\"`\n});\n```\n\nThe AI only sees state from components using `useAI`, keeping context focused and token usage efficient. The library waits for component re-renders before sending tool responses, ensuring the AI always receives the latest state.\n\n### Tool Definition with Zod\n\nTools are defined using Zod schemas for type safety and validation:\n\n```tsx\nconst addTodo = defineTool(\n  'Add a new todo item to the list',  // Description for AI\n  z.object({                          // Zod schema for input validation\n    text: z.string().describe('The text content of the todo item'),\n  }),\n  (input) => {                        // Typed implementation\n    // Your logic here\n    return { success: true, message: 'Todo added' };\n  }\n);\n```\n\nFor tools without arguments:\n\n```tsx\nconst logout = defineTool(\n  'Log the current user out',\n  () => {\n    // Logout logic\n  }\n);\n```\n\n### The `UseAIProvider`\n\nWrap your app to enable AI functionality:\n\n```tsx\nimport { UseAIProvider } from '@use-ai/client';\n\nroot.render(\n  <UseAIProvider\n    serverUrl=\"ws://localhost:8080\"\n    systemPrompt=\"Be concise and friendly in your responses.\"\n  >\n    <App />\n  </UseAIProvider>\n);\n```\n\nThe provider automatically aggregates tools from all `useAI` hooks in your component tree, providing a unified AI chat interface.\n\n## ✨ Key Features\n\n### Multi-Tool Use\n\nThe AI can call multiple tools in a single response, enabling emergent \"bulk action\" behavior:\n\n```tsx\nuseAI({\n  tools: { addTodo, deleteTodo },\n  prompt: `Todo List: ${JSON.stringify(todos)}`\n});\n```\n\nUser: *\"add a shopping list to make tonkotsu ramen\"*\n\nThe AI automatically calls `addTodo` multiple times for each ingredient, even though you only defined single-item operations.\n\n### Multiple Components of Same Type\n\nUse the `id` parameter to differentiate between component instances:\n\n```tsx\nfunction ListItem({ rowIndex, label, counter, color }) {\n  useAI({\n    tools: { updateLabel, incrementCounter, changeColor },\n    prompt: `Current state - Label: \"${label}\", Counter: ${counter}, Color: \"${color}\"`,\n    id: `Row ${rowIndex}`  // AI uses this to target specific rows\n  });\n}\n```\n\nOr use the component's `id` attribute:\n\n```tsx\n<ListItem id=\"Row 1\" />  // Automatically used as useAI id\n```\n\n### Invisible Components\n\nFor providers that expose tools but don't re-render:\n\n```tsx\nfunction EmailProvider({ apiKey }) {\n  useAI({\n    tools: { sendEmail },\n    invisible: true  // Don't wait for re-render after tool calls\n  });\n}\n```\n\n### Custom UI\n\nReplace the default chat interface with your own:\n\n```tsx\n<UseAIProvider\n  serverUrl=\"ws://localhost:8080\"\n  CustomButton={MyCustomButton}\n  CustomChat={MyCustomChat}\n>\n  <App />\n</UseAIProvider>\n```\n\n### Dangerous Actions Protection\n\nFor destructive operations, use `confirmationRequired`:\n\n```tsx\nconst deleteAccount = defineTool(\n  'Delete this user\\'s account permanently',\n  () => { /* deletion logic */ },\n  { confirmationRequired: true }\n);\n```\n\nThe AI will aggressively prompt for user confirmation before executing (note: not 100% foolproof as AI may still ignore).\n\n## 🏗️ Architecture\n\n### Data Flow\n\n```\n1. Tool Registration\n   Client → (register_tools) → Server\n\n2. User Prompt\n   User → Client → (prompt) → Server → Claude API\n\n3. Tool Execution\n   Claude API → (tool_use) → Server → (tool_call) → Client\n   Client executes tool locally\n   Client → (tool_response) → Server\n\n4. Final Response\n   Server → (tool_result) → Claude API → (response) → Server → Client\n```\n\n### Client-Side Tool Execution\n\nTools run **on the client** where application state lives, not on the server. The server acts as a coordinator between Claude API and client tools, enabling:\n\n- Direct access to React state\n- Browser API usage\n- Manipulation of local data\n- Zero backend dependencies for basic functionality\n\n### WebSocket Protocol\n\nAll messages follow `{ type: string, data: object }` format:\n\n**Client → Server:**\n- `register_tools`: Send tool definitions (JSON Schema)\n- `prompt`: Send user prompt\n- `tool_response`: Return tool execution result\n\n**Server → Client:**\n- `tool_call`: Request tool execution with `{ toolCallId, name, input }`\n- `response`: Final AI response\n- `error`: Error messages\n\n### Conversation History\n\nThe server maintains full conversation history per WebSocket session, enabling multi-turn conversations with context. History is session-based (in-memory) and lost on disconnect.\n\n### Prompt Caching\n\nAnthropic prompt caching is automatically used to reduce token costs and latency for repeated conversations.\n\n## 🔒 Security\n\n### Rate Limiting\n\nConfigure per-connection rate limits:\n\n```bash\nexport RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS=10\nexport RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000  # 1 minute\nbun run start:server\n```\n\nEach WebSocket connection is rate-limited independently to prevent abuse.\n\n### \"Lethal Trifecta\" Mitigations\n\nFollowing [Simon Willison's security guidance](https://simonwillison.net/2025/Jun/16/the-lethal-trifecta/), `use-ai` mitigates risks by:\n\n1. **Limited internet access**: Server cannot invoke tools to access external internet (no curl, search, etc.)\n2. **No backend API access**: V1 cannot access backend APIs directly\n3. **Client-only scope**: AI can only see what's on the page and take actions users can already perform\n\n### No Backend API Access (V1)\n\nThe current version intentionally does not provide backend API integration to minimize security risks and complexity. All tools must be implemented in frontend code.\n\n## 📊 Logging\n\nThe server supports two logging modes via `LOG_FORMAT` environment variable:\n\n### Pretty Logging (Development - Default)\n\nHuman-readable logs with formatting:\n```bash\nexport LOG_FORMAT=pretty\nbun run start:server\n```\n\nOutput:\n```\n━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\n📤 API REQUEST\n━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\n🔧 TOOLS: addTodo, deleteTodo, toggleTodo\n💬 MESSAGES: 3 total\n  1. [user] add a todo for groceries\n  2. [assistant] 2 content blocks\n  3. [user] 2 content blocks\n━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\n```\n\n### JSON Logging (Production)\n\nStructured logs for tools like Datadog, CloudWatch:\n```bash\nexport LOG_FORMAT=json\nbun run start:server\n```\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\"timestamp\":\"2025-01-06T10:30:45.123Z\",\"level\":\"info\",\"message\":\"API request\",\"tools\":[\"addTodo\"],\"messageCount\":3}\n```\n\n## 🧪 Testing\n\n### Unit Tests\n\n```bash\nbun test\n```\n\n### E2E Tests\n\nEnd-to-end tests using Playwright with real Claude API calls:\n\n```bash\nexport ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=your_api_key_here\n\nbun run test:e2e              # Run all E2E tests\nbun run test:e2e:ui           # Interactive UI mode\nbun run test:e2e:headed       # Visible browser mode\nbun run test:e2e:debug        # Debug mode\n```\n\nE2E tests are located in `apps/example/test/` and verify real AI interactions including edge cases.\n\n## 🏗️ Building\n\n```bash\nbun run build                # Build all packages\nbun run build:client         # Build @use-ai/client only\nbun run build:server         # Build @use-ai/server only\n```\n\nBuild process: Bun bundles code → TypeScript generates type declarations → ESM output\n\n## 🚢 Deployment\n\nThe server is designed to be deployed as a separate service on Kubernetes. It:\n\n- Has in-memory state only (active WebSocket sessions)\n- Can be horizontally scaled\n- Is shared across multiple applications\n- Requires only `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` environment variable\n\n## 📚 Tech Stack\n\n- **Runtime**: Bun\n- **Language**: TypeScript (strict mode)\n- **UI Framework**: React 18+\n- **AI Model**: Claude (Anthropic)\n- **Protocol**: WebSocket\n- **Schema Validation**: Zod\n- **Testing**: Bun test runner, Playwright\n\n## 🔄 Development Workflow\n\n1. Make changes to client or server packages\n2. Build packages: `bun run build:client` or `bun run build:server`\n3. Example app auto-reloads with `bun run dev`\n4. Run tests: `bun test` for unit tests, `bun run test:e2e` for E2E\n5. Verify in example app at http://localhost:3000\n\n## 📖 Examples\n\nSee `apps/example/` for a complete implementation featuring:\n- Todo list with add/delete/toggle operations\n- Multi-component state management\n- Real-time UI synchronization\n- Natural language interactions\n\n## 🤝 Contributing\n\nThis project uses:\n- Bun for package management and builds\n- TypeScript strict mode (never use `any` unless unavoidable)\n- ESM modules throughout\n- Conventional commit messages\n\n## 📄 License\n\n[License information to be added]\n",
