with Zod\n   107→\n   108→Tools are defined using Zod schemas for type safety and validation:\n   109→\n   110→```tsx\n   111→const addTodo = defineTool(\n   112→  'Add a new todo item to the list',  // Description for AI\n   113→  z.object({                          // Zod schema for input validation\n   114→    text: z.string().describe('The text content of the todo item'),\n   115→  }),\n   116→  (input) => {                        // Typed implementation\n   117→    // Your logic here\n   118→    return { success: true, message: 'Todo added' };\n   119→  }\n   120→);\n   121→```\n   122→\n   123→For tools without arguments:\n   124→\n   125→```tsx\n   126→const logout = defineTool(\n   127→  'Log the current user out',\n   128→  () => {\n   129→    // Logout logic\n   130→  }\n   131→);\n   132→```\n   133→\n   134→### The `UseAIProvider`\n   135→\n   136→Wrap your app to enable AI functionality:\n   137→\n   138→```tsx\n   139→import { UseAIProvider } from '@use-ai/client';\n   140→\n   141→root.render(\n   142→  <UseAIProvider\n   143→    serverUrl=\"ws://localhost:8080\"\n   144→    systemPrompt=\"Be concise and friendly in your responses.\"\n   145→  >\n   146→    <App />\n   147→  </UseAIProvider>\n   148→);\n   149→```\n   150→\n   151→The provider automatically aggregates tools from all `useAI` hooks in your component tree, providing a unified AI chat interface.\n   152→\n   153→## ✨ Key Features\n   154→\n   155→### Multi-Tool Use\n   156→\n   157→The AI can call multiple tools in a single response, enabling emergent \"bulk action\" behavior:\n   158→\n   159→```tsx\n   160→useAI({\n   161→  tools: { addTodo, deleteTodo },\n   162→  prompt: `Todo List: ${JSON.stringify(todos)}`\n   163→});\n   164→```\n   165→\n   166→User: *\"add a shopping list to make tonkotsu ramen\"*\n   167→\n   168→The AI automatically calls `addTodo` multiple times for each ingredient, even though you only defined single-item operations.\n   169→\n   170→### Multiple Components of Same Type\n   171→\n   172→Use the `id` parameter to differentiate between component instances:\n   173→\n   174→```tsx\n   175→function ListItem({ rowIndex, label, counter, color }) {\n   176→  useAI({\n   177→    tools: { updateLabel, incrementCounter, changeColor },\n   178→    prompt: `Current state - Label: \"${label}\", Counter: ${counter}, Color: \"${color}\"`,\n   179→    id: `Row ${rowIndex}`  // AI uses this to target specific rows\n   180→  });\n   181→}\n   182→```\n   183→\n   184→Or use the component's `id` attribute:\n   185→\n   186→```tsx\n   187→<ListItem id=\"Row 1\" />  // Automatically used as useAI id\n   188→```\n   189→\n   190→### Invisible Components\n   191→\n   192→For providers that expose tools but don't re-render:\n   193→\n   194→```tsx\n   195→function EmailProvider({ apiKey }) {\n   196→  useAI({\n   197→    tools: { sendEmail },\n   198→    invisible: true  // Don't wait for re-render after tool calls\n   199→  });\n   200→}\n   201→```\n   202→\n   203→### Custom UI\n   204→\n   205→Replace the default chat interface with your own:\n   206→\n   207→```tsx\n   208→<UseAIProvider\n   209→  serverUrl=\"ws://localhost:8080\"\n   210→  CustomButton={MyCustomButton}\n   211→  CustomChat={MyCustomChat}\n   212→>\n   213→  <App />\n   214→</UseAIProvider>\n   215→```\n   216→\n   217→### Dangerous Actions Protection\n   218→\n   219→For destructive operations, use `confirmationRequired`:\n   220→\n   221→```tsx\n   222→const deleteAccount = defineTool(\n   223→  'Delete this user\\'s account permanently',\n   224→  () => { /* deletion logic */ },\n   225→  { confirmationRequired: true }\n   226→);\n   227→```\n   228→\n   229→The AI will aggressively prompt for user confirmation before executing (note: not 100% foolproof as AI may still ignore).\n   230→\n   231→## 🏗️ Architecture\n   232→\n   233→### Data Flow\n   234→\n   235→```\n   236→1. Tool Registration\n   237→   Client → (register_tools) → Server\n   238→\n   239→2. User Prompt\n   240→   User → Client → (prompt) → Server → Claude API\n   241→\n   242→3. Tool Execution\n   243→   Claude API → (tool_use) → Server → (tool_call) → Client\n   244→   Client executes tool locally\n   245→   Client → (tool_response) → Server\n   246→\n   247→4. Final Response\n   248→   Server → (tool_result) → Claude API → (response) → Server → Client\n   249→```\n   250→\n   251→### Client-Side Tool Execution\n   252→\n   253→Tools run **on the client** where application state lives, not on the server. The server acts as a coordinator between Claude API and client tools, enabling:\n   254→\n   255→- Direct access to React state\n   256→- Browser API usage\n   257→- Manipulation of local data\n   258→- Zero backend dependencies for basic functionality\n   259→\n   260→### WebSocket Protocol\n   261→\n   262→All messages follow `{ type: string, data: object }` format:\n   263→\n   264→**Client → Server:**\n   265→- `register_tools`: Send tool definitions (JSON Schema)\n   266→- `prompt`: Send user prompt\n   267→- `tool_response`: Return tool execution result\n   268→\n   269→**Server → Client:**\n   270→- `tool_call`: Request tool execution with `{ toolCallId, name, input }`\n   271→- `response`: Final AI response\n   272→- `error`: Error messages\n   273→\n   274→### Conversation History\n   275→\n   276→The server maintains full conversation history per WebSocket session, enabling multi-turn conversations with context. History is session-based (in-memory) and lost on disconnect.\n   277→\n   278→### Prompt Caching\n   279→\n   280→Anthropic prompt caching is automatically used to reduce token costs and latency for repeated conversations.\n   281→\n   282→## 🔒 Security\n   283→\n   284→### Rate Limiting\n   285→\n   286→Configure per-connection rate limits:\n   287→\n   288→```bash\n   289→export RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS=10\n   290→export RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000  # 1 minute\n   291→bun run start:server\n   292→```\n   293→\n   294→Each WebSocket connection is rate-limited independently to prevent abuse.\n   295→\n   296→### \"Lethal Trifecta\" Mitigations\n   297→\n   298→Following [Simon Willison's security guidance](https://simonwillison.net/2025/Jun/16/the-lethal-trifecta/), `use-ai` mitigates risks by:\n   299→\n   300→1. **Limited internet access**: Server cannot invoke tools to access external internet (no curl, search, etc.)\n   301→2. **No backend API access**: V1 cannot access backend APIs directly\n   302→3. **Client-only scope**: AI can only see what's on the page and take actions users can already perform\n   303→\n   304→### No Backend API Access (V1)\n   305→\n   306→The current version intentionally does not provide backend API integration to minimize security risks and complexity. All tools must be implemented in frontend code.\n   307→\n   308→## 📊 Logging\n   309→\n   310→The server supports two logging modes via `LOG_FORMAT` environment variable:\n   311→\n   312→### Pretty Logging (Development - Default)\n   313→\n   314→Human-readable logs with formatting:\n   315→```bash\n   316→export LOG_FORMAT=pretty\n   317→bun run start:server\n   318→```\n   319→\n   320→Output:\n   321→```\n   322→━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\n   323→📤 API REQUEST\n   324→━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\n   325→🔧 TOOLS: addTodo, deleteTodo, toggleTodo\n   326→💬 MESSAGES: 3 total\n   327→  1. [user] add a todo for groceries\n   328→  2. [assistant] 2 content blocks\n   329→  3. [user] 2 content blocks\n   330→━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\n   331→```\n   332→\n   333→### JSON Logging (Production)\n   334→\n   335→Structured logs for tools like Datadog, CloudWatch:\n   336→```bash\n   337→export LOG_FORMAT=json\n   338→bun run start:server\n   339→```\n   340→\n   341→Output:\n   342→```json\n   343→{\"timestamp\":\"2025-01-06T10:30:45.123Z\",\"level\":\"info\",\"message\":\"API request\",\"tools\":[\"addTodo\"],\"messageCount\":3}\n   344→```\n   345→\n   346→## 🧪 Testing\n   347→\n   348→### Unit Tests\n   349→\n   350→```bash\n   351→bun test\n   352→```\n   353→\n   354→### E2E Tests\n   355→\n   356→End-to-end tests using Playwright with real Claude API calls:\n   357→\n   358→```bash\n   359→export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=your_api_key_here\n   360→\n   361→bun run test:e2e              # Run all E2E tests\n   362→bun run test:e2e:ui           # Interactive UI mode\n   363→bun run test:e2e:headed       # Visible browser mode\n   364→bun run test:e2e:debug        # Debug mode\n   365→```\n   366→\n   367→E2E tests are located in `apps/example/test/` and verify real AI interactions including edge cases.\n   368→\n   369→## 🏗️ Building\n   370→\n   371→```bash\n   372→bun run build                # Build all packages\n   373→bun run build:client         # Build @use-ai/client only\n   374→bun run build:server         # Build @use-ai/server only\n   375→```\n   376→\n   377→Build process: Bun bundles code → TypeScript generates type declarations → ESM output\n   378→\n   379→## 🚢 Deployment\n   380→\n   381→The server is designed to be deployed as a separate service on Kubernetes. It:\n   382→\n   383→- Has in-memory state only (active WebSocket sessions)\n   384→- Can be horizontally scaled\n   385→- Is shared across multiple applications\n   386→- Requires only `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` environment variable\n   387→\n   388→## 📚 Tech Stack\n   389→\n   390→- **Runtime**: Bun\n   391→- **Language**: TypeScript (strict mode)\n   392→- **UI Framework**: React 18+\n   393→- **AI Model**: Claude (Anthropic)\n   394→- **Protocol**: WebSocket\n   395→- **Schema Validation**: Zod\n   396→- **Testing**: Bun test runner, Playwright\n   397→\n   398→## 🔄 Development Workflow\n   399→\n   400→1. Make changes to client or server packages\n   401→2. Build packages: `bun run build:client` or `bun run build:server`\n   402→3. Example app auto-reloads with `bun run dev`\n   403→4. Run tests: `bun test` for unit tests, `bun run test:e2e` for E2E\n  